<h1>Chapter 3 — Constraint</h1>

<p class="lead">
  Constraint is not the enemy of freedom; it is the condition that makes real freedom possible.
</p>

<h2>I. The False Opposition Between Freedom and Limit</h2>

<p>
  Many people imagine that the strongest work emerges when all options remain open. In practice, the opposite is often true. When a maker is forced to decide within limits, attention becomes sharper and the resulting form becomes more exact.
</p>

<p>
  Constraint does not reduce creative possibility; it clarifies it. A narrow field of options often produces a more decisive gesture than an unlimited one.
</p>

<h2>II. Constraint as Structure</h2>

<p>
  A limit can be material, technical, economic, spatial, or conceptual. A board has a width. A room has a height. A budget has boundaries. A tradition has precedents. Each limit changes the form of the work before the maker even begins.
</p>

<p>
  What appears at first as restriction often reveals itself as structure. The maker is not being denied freedom; they are being given a grammar.
</p>

<h2>III. The Discipline of Selection</h2>

<p>
  The artist who works without constraint tends to accumulate. The artist who works with constraint learns to select. Every added feature must justify itself. Every gesture must carry consequence.
</p>

<p>
  This is why constraint so often yields clarity. It removes the noise of endless possibility and allows the essential form to stand.
</p>

<h2>IV. The Ethics of Limitation</h2>

<p>
  Limitations also shape character. A maker who cannot add everything can learn to leave things out. This discipline of omission is one of the most valuable habits in any practice because it trains judgment rather than appetite.
</p>

<p>
  The good work often reveals itself through the confidence of what it refuses.
</p>

